16.153.m:
The teacher, initiating him,
makes the Vedic student an embryo within;
he bears him three nights in his belly;
the gods assemble to see him when born.
The fathers, human beings, divine folks,
the gandharvas all follow this Vedic student.

17.2.m:
A great seat, great have you become;
great is your speed, your quivering, your trembling.
Indra protects you with manly vigor;
so make us shine forth, O earth,
as in the sight of gold.

Agni is in the earth, Agni in the plants;
the waters bear Agni, Agni is in the stones;
Agni is within human beings;
fires are in cattle, in horses.

17.3.m:
Clothed in fire, the earth has dark knees;
let her make me brilliant, sharpened.
On the earth they offer to the gods
the sacrifice, the oblation made ready;
on the earth human beings live,
mortals with svadhā-food and nourishment.
Let that earth bestow upon us breath, lifespan;
let the earth make me reach old age.

Pure waters flow for my body;
what impurity was mine, that we lay down;
with a strainer I purify the earth.

17.4.m:
Do not push me from behind, nor from the front;
make well-being for me, O earth.
Ward off the deadly weapon farther away;
let not highwaymen find [me].

Thrusting away, the cleansing one,
upon whom were the fires within the waters;
casting away the Dasyus, the god-revilers,
choosing Indra, the earth, not Vṛtra,
held fast to the mighty, the bullish male;
let him assign to us from the earth what wealth we desire.

17.5.m:
Upon whom two-footed birds fly together,
swans, eagles, falcons, birds;
upon whom the wind, Mātariśvan, speeds,
kicking up dusts and [shaking] the trees;
along the wind's blowing forth and blowing near the flame blows;
upon whom, assembled, black and ruddy,
day and night are set, over the earth—

17.6.m:
What I speak, let me speak that full of honey;
what I look upon, let the pure streams purify me;
I am full of brilliance, full of speed; I strike down those who rage.

17.10.m:
Whose measure is the earth, and the atmosphere his belly;
who made the sky his head—
to that eldest brahman, homage!

Skambha held fast the earth and yonder sky;
Skambha held fast the broad atmosphere;
Skambha held fast the six broad directions;
Skambha entered into this whole world.

17.11.m:
Smit away is his darkness;
he is separated from evil;
in him are all the lights,
the three that are in Prajāpati.

He does not surpass Prajāpati;
he does not surpass the Most High;
and also there he is not veiled for us,
which, the eldest, passed beyond.

It is indeed beyond the earth;
it is indeed beyond the sky;
the worlds indeed are woven together in that
in which these creatures are woven.

17.15.m:
Those who are maidens, those who are old,
those Sadānvas who are young women—
let all the capped ones depart;
let the cow belonging to the family crawl away;
away from here, you Arāyī monstrosities!

Against them let Rudra discharge
his brightness that slays travellers on the way.
Let the archer slay them with lightning,
with an unswerving thunderbolt;
O mighty Indra, do not leave a remnant of them;
pound the chatterers to chaff!

17.16.m:
Through a hornless [cow] his [limbs] are shattered;
through a lame one he tumbles into a ditch;
through a tailless one his houses are burned;
through a one-eyed one his own [wealth] is destroyed.

As she is born, the barren cow is born
for the gods together with the brāhmaṇas;
therefore she is to be given to the brāhmaṇas;
that, they say, is the preservation of one’s own.

17.19.m:
She resolves in her mind;
that reaches unto the gods;
and further, the brāhmaṇas approach
to ask for the barren cow.

The barren cow is your mother, O rājanya;
so was it brought to pass from the beginning;
they speak of non-surrender of her
who is given forth to the brāhmaṇas.

17.22.m:
Those who performed austerity, who brought forth austerity—
to them, the austerity-performers, the austerity-fashioners, that […];
let them, the austerity-performers, the austerity-fashioners, that […]!

17.23.m:
I strike him; I pierce him with bad dreaming;
I pierce him with shortness of life;
I pierce him with consumption of life-span;
I drive him downward away toward the path of death!

17.24.m:
We know your birthplace, O Sleep:
you are the son of Evil, born from Non-existence, the agent of Yama.
As such we know you, O Sleep.
What is good sleep, that is mine;
what is evil, that we send forth to the hater.
We make that reach him;
with it we pierce him;
with non-existence we pierce him;
with destruction we pierce him;
with downfall we pierce him;
with the Seizer we pierce him;
with darkness we pierce him;
into the flesh-eating fire we cut him off;
we dispatch him against the dread, cruel [messengers] of the gods;
we place him between the jaws of Vaiśvānara!

17.25.m:
Both Heaven and the days have extended themselves there;
no godless man obscures your ancient [light].
When you drive your chariot forward with your Etaśas,
one dark space follows behind;
with another light you rise, O Sūrya!

17.35.m:
These paths leading to the gods indeed are what the rays of the sun are; he […].

His two eyes are sun and moon;
his blinkings are days and nights;
his bellies are the rays of the sun; he […].

17.49.m:
Cling behind the draft-ox, the raft;
he will carry you across from distress, from reproach.
Mount the golden ship of Savitṛ;
with six billows may we cross beyond lack of thought.

17.50.m:
Take hold toward the eastern quarter;
to that world those full of trust attach themselves.
Measure out the vessel; let it be full for you two;
may that cooked [offering] guide you two on the path leading to the fathers.

17.51.m:
Lead us to the heavenly world;
may we be together with wife, together with sons.
I take her hand: let her follow here;
let not Nirṛti overcome us, nor malevolence.

17.52.m:
Distinct are the forms of animals in many ways;
of one form it becomes in complete success.
Cast off this red skin;
the pressing-stone will cleanse it like a washerman clothes.

Let Agni cooking protect you from the front;
let Indra protect from the south by his greatness.
Let Soma make you firm in the foundation from the west;
from the north let Varuṇa unite you.

Let them come from the sky: they attend the earth;
from the earth they attend the wide intermediate space.
Being pure, they indeed cleanse;
let them lead us to the heavenly world.

17.53.m:
Spread fresh sacrificial grass for the rice-mess;
let it be pleasing to the heart, lovely to the eye.
Let the gods enter there together with the gods;
seated on the south, let them eat of this.

For sixty autumns we entrust this [mess];
may he attain heaven through the cooked [food].
Unto him sons and fathers […];
O Agni, make him go to this heavenly world.

You have spread [it]; you have made this world;
broad and wide, an unequalled heaven.
Upon it let the great eagle rest;
let the gods offer this to the deities.

17.54.m:
Let the two fires burn the demon that is godless;
let not the flesh-eating Piśāca drink here.
We thrust him away, we keep him off from us;
may the Ādityas, the Aṅgirases attend us.

I cook, I alone speak;
even the wives take part in my sacred work.
A world of youth, a son has been born;
take hold of life that brings advancement.

17.55.m:
This [form] was born among skins upon man;
all other beasts are not naked.
Clothe yourselves with royal power;
the unwove garment is the surface of the rice-mess.

To the eastern quarter, to Agni as sovereign lord, to Asita as protector,
to the Āditya with arrows we entrust this [mess].
Let them guard it for us until our arrival;
let what is destined for us here lead us to old age.
May old age entrust us to death;
then may we be united with the cooked [offering].

To the upward quarter, to Bṛhaspati as sovereign lord, to Śvitra as protector,
to the lightning-bolts with arrows we entrust this [mess].
Let them guard it for us until our arrival;
let what is destined for us here lead us to old age.
May old age entrust us to death;
then may we be united with the cooked [offering].

18.1.m:
Supported by truth is the earth;
supported by the sun is heaven.
By cosmic order the Ādityas stand;
in heaven Soma has taken his seat.

The Raibhī verse was her bridal companion;
the Nārāśaṃsī verse was her attendant.
Sūryā's garment was fair;
she goes with song […].

The chants were the cross-bars;
the meter was the head-dress […].
The Aśvins were Sūryā's suitors;
Agni was the harbinger.

Mind was her cart;
heaven was the canopy;
two bright oxen were harnessed,
when Sūryā went to her husband.

18.2.m:
Let Bhaga lead you hence, taking your hand;
let the Aśvins convey you in their chariot.
Go to the house, that you may be mistress of the house;
ruling, you will speak to the council.

Here let what is dear succeed for your offspring;
keep watch in this house for the mastership of the house.
Unite your body with this husband;
then, grown old, you will speak to the council.

18.3.m:
These two wander through illusion, one before, one after;
two children playing, they circle round the sacrifice.
The one surveys all beings;
the other, ordaining the seasons, is born anew.

Ever new he becomes as he is born;
banner of the days, he goes at the head of the dawns.
Coming, he apportions their share to the gods;
the moon prolongs a long life.

It becomes dark blue and red;
the magic attachment is smeared off.
Her kinsmen prosper;
the husband is bound in fetters.

18.4.m:
Let the brāhmaṇas remove her heating [garment];
let the waters draw off what slays men.
Let her quickly go round the fire of Aryaman;
the father-in-law and the brother-in-law await her.

Auspicious to you be the gold, and auspicious be the waters;
auspicious be the post, auspicious the aperture of the yoke;
auspicious to you be the waters of a hundred purifiers;
auspiciously unite your body with your husband.

18.5.m:
May the god Savitṛ take your hand;
may King Soma make you one with good offspring;
may Agni Jātavedas make you fortunate,
reach old age as a wife with your husband.

Dhātṛ has seized your hand;
Savitṛ has seized your hand;
Bhaga has seized your hand;
Aryaman has seized your hand.
You are the wife by dharma;
I am your master of the house.

18.6.m:
May the houses envelop you with splendor, with Bhaga;
put on this bright form,
like Dawn at the sight of the sun.

Lift [her] up! You shall strike away the rakṣas;
place this woman in good action.
Dhātṛ, the wise, has found a husband for her;
let king Bhaga, knowing, go before.

Bhaga fashioned the four feet;
Bhaga fashioned the four frames;
Tvaṣṭṛ adorned the straps in the middle.
May she be good-omened for us.

18.7.m:
You were Soma's wife at first;
the Gandharva was your next husband;
Agni was your third husband;
your fourth is human-born.

Soma gave [her] to the Gandharva;
the Gandharva gave [her] to Agni;
wealth and sons Agni gave
to me, and then this woman.

May the goodwill of you two come to us, O possessors of prized wealth;
O Aśvins, the desires have settled in our hearts.
Become protectors, a pair, O lords of beauty;
may we reach the dear dwellings of Aryaman.

18.8.m:
Stand firm like Virāj,
like Viṣṇu here, O Sarasvatī;
like dūrvā-grass, O Indra, may she bring forth progeny;
may she be in the goodwill of Bhaga.

May the surge strike upward the two yoke-pins;
may the waters loosen the harness-straps;
may the two cows that are not ill-doers, free from sin,
reach the height without injury.

18.9.m:
Mount the hide, sit down by the fire;
this god slays all the rakṣases.
Good-omened, sit by this fire;
as mistress of the house, attend upon the gods here.

18.10.m:
Rise up from here, for this one has a husband;
I praise Viśvāvasu with homage and songs.
Seek a maiden residing with her father, clinging [to her kin];
that is your share by birth, know that!

Homage to the mind of the Gandharva;
homage to his anger and his eye we pay.
O Viśvāvasu, I pay homage to you with a sacred formulation;
go away to the apsarases, your wives!

May we be well-disposed through wealth;
we have turned the Gandharva away from here.
That god has gone to the highest abode;
we have gone forward, prolonging our lifespan.

Unite, parents, at the proper season;
may you become father and mother of semen.
O youth, mount this young woman;
produce offspring here, rejoicing together!

May Prajāpati generate offspring for you two;
may Aryaman anoint [you] together with day and night.
Not ill-omened, enter this world of your husband;
bring wellbeing to our two-footed, bring wellbeing to our four-footed!
